{#- modules/identity/ema.j2
    Enterprise-Managed Authorization for MCP.

    Sources, in order of authority: the EMA extension spec
    (modelcontextprotocol.io/extensions/auth/enterprise-managed-authorization,
    promoted to Stable 2026-06-17); draft-ietf-oauth-identity-assertion-authz-grant-04
    (ID-JAG, an IETF WG draft); RFC 8693 (token exchange, leg 1), RFC 7523
    (jwt-bearer, leg 2), RFC 9728 (protected resource metadata); the MCP base
    authorization spec (2025-06-18) for what the MCP server must check. The
    problem slide's wording is the EMA docs page's own "What it is" list.

    What the diagram asserts, and where it comes from:
      - Leg 1 is a token exchange at the ENTERPRISE IdP (EMA §4): ID token in,
        ID-JAG out. ID-JAG claims (draft §3.1): typ oauth-id-jag+jwt, `aud` = the
        Resource Authorization Server's issuer, `resource` = the MCP server
        (RFC 8707 form), `client_id` = the client's id AT THE RESOURCE AS (may
        differ from its IdP id), `jti` unique, `scope` as granted by admin policy
        (EMA §4.1, §7.2). Response token_type is "N_A" (EMA §4.2). Neither spec
        says the ID-JAG is single-use.
      - Leg 2 presents the ID-JAG as the `assertion` of a jwt-bearer grant at the
        Resource (vendor's) Authorization Server (EMA §5), which already trusts the
        enterprise IdP as issuer (draft §2.1) and mints an access token that MUST be
        audience-restricted to the MCP server (EMA §5.1). The client authenticates
        there with registered credentials or a Client ID Metadata Document (EMA §5).
      - The MCP server MUST reject tokens not issued for it (MCP base auth spec,
        "Token Audience Binding and Validation"). Issuer/scope checks are ordinary
        resource-server behaviour, not an EMA MUST.
      - No redirect to the vendor AS's authorization endpoint and no user-approval
        step there (draft §3). SSO to the enterprise IdP still happens in a browser
        (EMA §3).

    Import WITH CONTEXT.
      {%- from "modules/identity/ema.j2" import problem, morph with context %}
-#}

{%- from "modules/branding/" ~ branding ~ "/primitives.j2" import
    content_slide, slide_heading, numbered_steps
-%}
{%- from "modules/identity/morph-kit.j2" import picto, sentinels, crumbs, styles, stage_pages, arrowhead, ah, num -%}

{%- macro problem() -%}
{%- call content_slide("Per-server OAuth") %}
  {{ slide_heading("", "Traditional MCP") }}
  {#- Titles only. The EMA docs page's own wording for each is in the header
      comment (onboarding one service at a time, offboarding per service, no
      consistent policy, no visibility into which servers are in use). -#}
  {#- Four title-only rows read better with bigger numerals and more air; the
      pack's defaults are tuned for rows that carry a detail line. -#}
  <style>
    .ema-prob { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
    .ema-prob .w-12 { width: 4.25rem; height: 4.25rem; }
    .ema-prob .w-12 span { font-size: 2.2rem; }
    .ema-prob .pb-7 { padding-bottom: 4rem; }
    .ema-prob .pt-1\.5 { padding-top: 1.1rem; }
  </style>
  <div class="ema-prob">
  {{ numbered_steps([
    {"title": "Each employee authorizes each MCP server, individually", "accent": "danger"},
    {"title": "Offboarding is per service", "accent": "warning"},
    {"title": "No consistent policy", "accent": "info"},
    {"title": "No visibility or control over which servers are in use", "accent": "secondary"},
  ], reveal="focus") }}
  </div>
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro morph() -%}
{%- call content_slide("Enterprise-Managed Authorization") %}
{{ styles("ema", 4, moves=[
  {"cls": "ema-policy", "from": "translate(1412px, 106px)", "at": 1, "to": "translate(305px, 106px)"},
], pages=true) }}
{%- call(stage) stage_pages("ema", 4) %}
  {{ sentinels("ema", 4, stage) }}
  {{ slide_heading("", "Enterprise-Managed Authorization (EMA)") }}
  {{ crumbs("ema", ["Per-server OAuth", "Leg 1: ID-JAG", "Leg 2: access token", "The call"], stage) }}

  <svg viewBox="0 -15 1600 665" role="img"
       aria-label="Architecture diagram that steps from per-server OAuth to Enterprise-Managed Authorization: the policy decision moves from the vendor's consent screen to the enterprise identity provider, which issues an ID-JAG that the vendor's authorization server redeems for an audience-bound access token.">
    {{ arrowhead("ema", stage) }}

    {#- ── arrows ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -#}

    {#- Developer → MCP client, today: the human is the policy decision point -#}
    <g class="mk-arrow ema-v on0 bad0">
      <path d="M309,500 L701,500" marker-end="{{ ah("ema", stage) }}"/>
      <text x="505" y="484" text-anchor="middle">logs in and consents, per server</text>
    </g>

    {#- Developer ↕ Enterprise IdP: SSO once -#}
    <g class="mk-arrow ema-v on1 on2 on3 hot1">
      <path d="M250,240 L250,440" marker-start="{{ ah("ema", stage) }}" marker-end="{{ ah("ema", stage) }}"/>
      <text x="264" y="346">{{ num("&#9312;", "login, get ID token") }}</text>
    </g>

    {#- MCP client ↔ Enterprise IdP: leg 1 -#}
    <g class="mk-arrow ema-v on1 on2 on3 hot1">
      <path d="M722,447 L298,150" marker-start="{{ ah("ema", stage) }}" marker-end="{{ ah("ema", stage) }}"/>
      <text x="566" y="330">{{ num("&#9313;", "ID token &#8594; ID-JAG") }}</text>
    </g>

    {#- MCP client ↔ Vendor AS: per-server OAuth today, leg 2 under EMA -#}
    <g class="mk-arrow ema-v on0 on2 on3 hot2">
      <path d="M812,447 L1291,140" marker-start="{{ ah("ema", stage) }}" marker-end="{{ ah("ema", stage) }}"/>
      <text class="ema-v on2 on3" x="1040" y="268" text-anchor="end">{{ num("&#9314;", "ID-JAG &#8594; access token") }}</text>
    </g>

    {#- Vendor AS → Enterprise IdP, today: the vendor's login federates to the
        enterprise IdP for authentication (SSO). The IdP signs people in but has
        no part in the authorization decision. Deployment practice, not EMA. -#}
    <g class="mk-arrow ema-v on0">
      <path class="dashed" d="M1297,70 C1297,-20 298,-20 298,78" marker-end="{{ ah("ema", stage) }}"/>
      <text x="800" y="44" text-anchor="middle">SSO login</text>
    </g>

    {#- Vendor AS → Enterprise IdP: the trust boundary, configured once -#}
    <g class="mk-arrow ema-v on2 on3 hot2">
      <path class="dashed" d="M1297,70 C1297,-20 298,-20 298,78" marker-end="{{ ah("ema", stage) }}"/>
      <text x="800" y="44" text-anchor="middle">trusts the enterprise IdP</text>
    </g>

    {#- MCP server → Vendor AS: validation -#}
    <g class="mk-arrow ema-v on0 on3">
      <path d="M1350,240 L1350,440" marker-end="{{ ah("ema", stage) }}"/>
      <text x="1364" y="346">validates</text>
    </g>

    {#- MCP client → MCP server: the call -#}
    <g class="mk-arrow ema-v on0 on3 bad0 hot3">
      <path d="M818,482 L1296,482" marker-end="{{ ah("ema", stage) }}"/>
      <text class="ema-v on0" x="1057" y="466" text-anchor="middle">Bearer token</text>
      <text class="ema-v on3" x="1057" y="466" text-anchor="middle">{{ num("&#9315;", "normal access token") }}</text>
    </g>

    {#- ── parties ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -#}

    <g class="mk-party side-p" transform="translate(250,500)">
      {{ picto("person") }}
      <text class="t" y="85">Developer</text>
    </g>

    <g class="mk-party side-a" transform="translate(760,500)">
      {{ picto("ide") }}
      <text class="t" y="85">MCP Client</text>
    </g>

    <g class="mk-party side-r hot3" transform="translate(1350,500)">
      {{ picto("rack") }}
      <text class="t" y="85">MCP Server</text>
    </g>

    <g class="mk-party side-p hot1" transform="translate(250,120)">
      {{ picto("badge") }}
      <text class="t" y="85">Enterprise IdP</text>
    </g>

    <g class="mk-party side-r dim1 hot2" transform="translate(1350,120)">
      {{ picto("login") }}
      <text class="t" y="85">Vendor&rsquo;s Auth Server</text>
      <g class="mk-tag ema-v on0" transform="translate(62,14)">
        <rect width="100" height="24" rx="12"/>
        <text x="50" y="16.5" text-anchor="middle">consent</text>
      </g>
    </g>

    {#- The policy badge: at the vendor until press 1, then it slides to the
        enterprise IdP. That slide is the whole point of EMA. -#}
    <g class="mk-tag ema-policy hot1">
      <rect width="90" height="24" rx="12"/>
      <text x="45" y="16.5" text-anchor="middle">policy</text>
    </g>
  </svg>
{%- endcall %}
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}
